BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sphera-media-lab-2026-open-call-discourse@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sphera Media Lab 2026: Open Call for Masterclasses and Ke
 ynotes
DESCRIPTION:European Lab and the Sphera Network are opening submissions for
  the discourse programme of the next Sphera Media Lab\, the annual forum f
 or people defending independent media in Europe. THEMES: technological ind
 ependence (sovereignty\, tools\, mutualisation)\; economic independence (c
 ooperative models)\; editorial independence (press freedom\, resisting the
  far right). FORMATS SOUGHT: masterclasses\, led by an expert sharing adva
 nced knowledge\, technique and hands-on insight\; and keynotes\, inspiring
  talks grounded in a specific case study. WHO CAN PROPOSE: media outlets\,
  associations\, researchers and professionals. The organisers state they p
 ay particular attention to parity and diversity across their programmes an
 d ask that proposals take those criteria into account. WHAT IS COVERED: se
 lected contributors travel to Brussels with transport\, accommodation and 
 a fee covered. THE EVENT: Sphera Media Lab 2026 runs on 3 December 2026 at
  LaVallee in Brussels\, a full day of conferences\, masterclasses and work
 shops for independent media professionals from across Europe\, alongside t
 he premiere of a documentary on threats facing European journalists. APPLI
 CATION: submit your masterclass or keynote proposal via the Typeform at ar
 ty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM before 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 European Lab (Arty Farty) and the Sphera Network\, coordinated by Medianes
 \nLocation: LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. 
 Proposals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should
  reflect a European perspective.\nAward: Selected contributors receive a f
 ee plus travel to Brussels and accommodation\, all covered by the organise
 rs. The fee amount is not published in the call. No submission fee.\nDurat
 ion: One-day event on 3 December 2026\; contributors deliver a single mast
 erclass or keynote\n\nMore: https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. Prop
 osals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should ref
 lect a European perspective.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-international-collaborative-projects-heritage-institutes-2026
 @artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: International Collaborative Projects with
  Heritage Institutes (September 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:This scheme funds cultural heritage institutions in the Netherl
 ands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom that want to collaborate with f
 oreign heritage institutions on research projects in shared collection are
 as\, including art historical and provenance research. A development phase
  can also be funded\, covering travel\, accommodation and presentation cos
 ts while the collaboration is being shaped. CONDITIONS: the applicant inst
 itution must be publicly accessible and manage nationally significant cult
 ural heritage\; it must partner with comparable institutions abroad\; the 
 research must be presented by both the Dutch and the international partner
 s\; the applicant must contribute significantly to financing the project\;
  and the international partners' share of costs must be proportional to th
 e Mondriaan Fund grant. AMOUNTS: up to 40% of variable project costs for t
 he main project\, or up to 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000 for
  development research. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund online application
  system after registering an account. Deadline 10 September 2026 before 16
 :00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time (an earlier 28 May 2026 round has pa
 ssed).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\nLocation: Applicant institutions m
 ust be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; the
  partner institutions are abroad.\nAward: Determined per application. Up t
 o 40% of variable project costs. For a development research phase\, up to 
 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000\, covering travel\, accommodat
 ion and presentation costs. The applicant must contribute significantly to
  the project financing\, and the international partners' cost share must b
 e proportional to the Mondriaan Fund grant. No application fee.\nDuration:
  Project-based research collaboration. Two annual rounds\; this card cover
 s the 10 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/e
 n/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international-collaborative-projects-with-herit
 age-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international
 -collaborative-projects-with-heritage-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant institutions must be based in the Netherlands or the Car
 ibbean part of the Kingdom\; the partner institutions are abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-research-networking-day-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM 2027 Research Networking Day: Open Call for Presentat
 ions (Berlin)
DESCRIPTION:The Research Networking Day (RND) is an exchange platform for g
 raduate or postgraduate students and independent artists conducting self-g
 uided research traversing audio\, arts\, media\, design\, and related theo
 retical disciplines. The RND 2027 open call seeks innovative and critical 
 submissions from all areas of study addressing the CTM 2027 festival theme
  'Process...ing'. Nine selected candidates give short ten-minute presentat
 ions within different modules\, linked by discussion rounds at the end of 
 each module\, offering an international platform to exchange with colleagu
 es and researchers working on related ideas\; research is also shared via 
 audio/video after the festival on CTM's YouTube and SoundCloud channels. T
 his edition takes place in collaboration with the Design Academy Eindhoven
 \, Universitat der Kunste Berlin (UdK)\, and Leuphana University Luneburg.
  CONDITIONS: presentations take place on-site within the festival's Discou
 rse programme\, in English\; applications are for individual (solo) presen
 tations only (no duos or groups). As in previous years\, no funding is ava
 ilable for travel and accommodation\; participants receive a CTM 2027 fest
 ival pass\, a catered lunch on the day\, and a group dinner. Entrance to t
 he event is free. KEY DATES: application deadline 25 October 2026 at 23:59
  CET\; programme announced mid to late November 2026\; RND 2027 event Sund
 ay 24 January 2027\; CTM 2027 runs 22 to 31 January 2027.\n\nOrganisation:
  CTM Festival\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. On-site presentations within th
 e CTM festival's Discourse programme\, in English.\nAward: No funding for 
 travel or accommodation. Participants receive a CTM 2027 festival pass\, a
  catered lunch on the day of presentations\, and a group dinner. Entrance 
 to the event is free\; free to apply.\nDuration: One-day event\, Sunday 24
  January 2027 (within CTM 2027\, 22-31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.
 ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/research-networking-day-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/research-networkin
 g-day-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. On-site presentations within the CTM festival's 
 Discourse programme\, in English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nwo-ssh-xs-2026-round-3@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: NWO: SSH Open Competition XS 2026 - Round 3
DESCRIPTION:Small curiosity-driven research grants in the social sciences a
 nd humanities\, awarded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO). Round 3 of th
 e 2026 SSH Open Competition XS closes 15 September 2026\, 14:00 CEST. Up t
 o EUR 50\,000 per project to enable researchers to pursue novel\, explorat
 ory and high-risk ideas without the constraints of larger themed calls. EL
 IGIBILITY: academic researchers with an institutional affiliation at a Dut
 ch university or recognised Dutch knowledge institute. Proposals are asses
 sed on scientific quality and the potential for renewal of social-sciences
  and humanities research. Round 3 details and the dedicated application pa
 ge will be published by NWO closer to the deadline\; the linked page is th
 e most recent SSH XS round\, which is the same scheme.\n\nOrganisation: Du
 tch Research Council (NWO)\nLocation: Netherlands (applicants must be acad
 emic researchers affiliated to a Dutch knowledge institution)\nAward: Up t
 o EUR 50\,000 per project\nDuration: Project-based\, typically 12-24 month
 s\n\nMore: https://www.nwo.nl/en/calls/ssh-open-competition-xs-2026-round-
 3?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nwo.nl/en/calls/ssh-open-competition-xs-2026-round-3?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands (applicants must be academic researchers affiliated to
  a Dutch knowledge institution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cais-research-sabbaticals-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260723
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:Deadline: CAIS Research Sabbaticals (Fellowships) 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:Residential research sabbaticals at CAIS\, explicitly open to e
 xcellent scholars AND practitioners across all career stages and disciplin
 es (not academia-only). Funds individual projects on the societal impact o
 f digital transformation\, including pure research and applied projects th
 at develop new theories\, methods or perspectives for practice. Project mu
 st be self-contained with specific milestones and produce an independent o
 utput suitable for short-timeframe publication: peer-reviewed paper or con
 ference contribution\, book chapter\, policy paper\, or prototype. Fellows
  join a vibrant interdisciplinary research community with regular joint ac
 tivities (breakfast Tuesdays\, colloquium and dinner Wednesdays\, occasion
 al workshops Thursdays) and an international network of alumni\, working g
 roups and affiliates. In Germany\, full and compensatory grants are not su
 bject to social security contributions and are usually tax-exempt\; fellow
 s resident abroad should verify their own tax position. Note: CAIS is curr
 ently reviewing the application format and selection process for the next 
 call\, so details may change. Next call publishes at the beginning of June
  2026\; deadline 23 July 2026 for fellowships in the period October 2027 t
 o March 2028. Contact: Dr. Esther Laufer\, esther.laufer@cais-research.de.
 \n\nOrganisation: Center for Advanced Internet Studies (CAIS)\, Bochum\, G
 ermany\nLocation: Bochum\, Germany (residential at CAIS\; rent-free\, full
 y furnished apartment provided plus private office)\nAward: Sabbatical lea
 ve on full salary: additional €600/month grant. No regular income: €2\
 ,000/month grant. Regular income below €1\,400/month: top-up to the full
  grant. Alternative: reimbursement of salary or substitute costs within re
 asonable limits. €100/month extra per child under 18 for fellows on a fu
 ll or compensatory grant. CAIS also covers one return trip to Bochum (or a
  daily commute if local)\, provides a rent-free fully furnished apartment 
 and a private office. Fellows can request financial support for research e
 xpenses\, invite a Visiting Fellow for up to 3 weeks of collaboration\, an
 d invite up to three European experts for half-day workshops (CAIS covers 
 travel\, accommodation and a daily allowance of up to €24).\nDuration: 3
  or 6 months. 6-month fellowships usually start in October or April\; 3-mo
 nth fellowships start in October\, January\, April or July. Exceptions pos
 sible by arrangement.\n\nMore: https://www.cais-research.de/en/cais-colleg
 e/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cais-research.de/en/cais-college/fellowships/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bochum\, Germany (residential at CAIS\; rent-free\, fully furnishe
 d apartment provided plus private office)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T145251Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowships 2026 (MS
 CA-PF)
DESCRIPTION:MSCA Postdoctoral Fellowships support researchers holding a PhD
  who wish to carry out research abroad\, acquire new skills\, develop thei
 r careers and have international mobility. Open to excellent researchers o
 f any nationality. The 2026 call opened 9 April 2026 and closes 9 Septembe
 r 2026 at 17:00 CEST\; notification of call results expected February 2027
 \; grant agreement signature April 2027. Two strands: European Postdoctora
 l Fellowships (12 to 24 months in Europe) and Global Postdoctoral Fellowsh
 ips (12 to 24 months outside Europe + 12-month return phase in Europe). Th
 e project must take place in a country different from where the researcher
  has worked or studied. Approximately 1\,600 projects funded. Apply via th
 e EU Funding & Tenders Opportunities Portal\; submission is by the fellow 
 plus host institution. Eligibility: researcher must have a PhD at the call
  deadline (or have submitted thesis with all requirements met)\, maximum 8
  years full-time-equivalent research experience post-PhD\, must comply wit
 h the mobility rule (no more than 12 months in the host country in the 36 
 months before the deadline).\n\nOrganisation: European Commission (Marie S
 kłodowska-Curie Actions\, under Horizon Europe\; managed by the European 
 Research Executive Agency\, REA)\nLocation: Europe (host institution in an
  EU Member State or Horizon Europe Associated Country\; also possible: Glo
 bal Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted in non-associated third countries with
  a return phase in Europe)\nAward: Standard MSCA unit costs covering resea
 rcher allowance (living\, mobility\, family)\, research/training/networkin
 g costs and management/indirect costs (annual values published in the call
 's Work Programme\; typically a fully-funded postdoc package of roughly 
 €5\,000 to €8\,000+ per researcher per month equivalent depending on h
 ost country correction coefficient)\nDuration: 12 to 24 months (European P
 ostdoctoral Fellowships)\; Global Postdoctoral Fellowships are 24 to 36 mo
 nths including a 12-month return phase in Europe\n\nMore: https://marie-sk
 lodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-
 2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://marie-sklodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdo
 ctoral-fellowships-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Europe (host institution in an EU Member State or Horizon Europe A
 ssociated Country\; also possible: Global Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted 
 in non-associated third countries with a return phase in Europe)
END:VEVENT
